<item>
  <title>Two new papers in Computational Mechanics</title>
  <link>https://alexandredabyseesaram.github.io/posts/2025/2025-08-30-Papers_CM/</link>
  <description><![CDATA[ 





<p><a href="https://mgenet.github.io/">Martin Genet</a>, <a href="https://m3disim.saclay.inria.fr/people/katerina-skardova/">Katerina Skardova</a> and I just published two papers in <a href="https://link.springer.com/journal/466"><em>Computational Mechanics</em></a>, entitled</p>
<ul>
<li><a href="https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s00466-025-02677-3">Finite element neural network interpolation: Part I—interpretable and adaptive discretization for solving PDEs</a>, and</li>
<li><a href="https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s00466-025-02676-4">Finite element neural network interpolation: Part II—hybridisation with the proper generalised decomposition for non-linear surrogate modelling</a>.</li>
</ul>
<p>All computations from the paper (which rely on NeuROM <span class="citation" data-cites="daby-seesaramNeuROM2024a">(Daby-Seesaram et al. 2025)</span>) are easily reproducible at</p>
<ul>
<li><a href="https://alexandredabyseesaram.github.io/FENNI-I-paper-demo/">https://alexandredabyseesaram.github.io/FENNI-I-paper-demo</a>, and</li>
<li><a href="https://alexandredabyseesaram.github.io/FENNI-II-PGD-paper-demo">https://alexandredabyseesaram.github.io/FENNI-II-PGD-paper-demo</a>,</li>
</ul>
<p>so do not hesitate to give it a try, and let us know how it goes!</p>
